# Part 2 — Two defects found while tracing the path

Neither is a rendering bug. Both are places where the UI asserts liveness it does
not have.

## 2.1 Defect A — the module set is frozen after the first poll

`catalogShape()` (`catalog.ts:960`) is a pure function of the module-level
`CATALOG` constant:

```ts
export function catalogShape(): string {
  const parts: string[] = [];
  for (const e of CATALOG) {
    parts.push(`${e.id}:${e.order}:${e.radius}:${e.arc}:${e.speed}:${e.channels.length}`);
  }
  return parts.join('|');
}
```

It takes no arguments and reads no query results, so `catalogRevision` is a
**compile-time constant** for the lifetime of the process.

The module list it is supposed to fingerprint is not constant. `assembleSnapshot`
returns `[...modules, ...overflow]` (`assemble.ts:446`), where:

- `expandCatalog` (`assemble.ts:272`) turns the single `probe` entry into one
  module per distinct `instance` in `probeSuccess` — ten today, one per blackbox
  target.
- `buildOverflow` (`assemble.ts:343`) appends a module for every `up` row with no
  catalogue home, capped at `MAX_OVERFLOW = 64`.

On the client, `useLive.ts:119-126` memoises `catalogOf(snapshot)` on `[revision]`
with `snapshot` deliberately excluded from the dependency array, and
`useModules.ts:94` early-returns when `catalogRevision === applied.current`. So
`catalogOf` runs **exactly once per page load**, against the first snapshot that
arrives.

Consequences, in order of how likely they are to be noticed:

- A blackbox probe added to the cluster never appears as a strip.
- A scrape target that disappears never leaves the scene; its strip falls back to
  the authored status baked in at first poll and sits there.
- A newly discovered exporter never reaches the overflow ring.
- If `probeSuccess` fails on the *first* snapshot, `expandCatalog` skips the entry
  entirely at `assemble.ts:289-291` and all ten probe modules are absent for the
  rest of the session.
- `reset()` (`useModules.ts:231`) restores the same frozen list, so the obvious
  recovery gesture does not recover.

Only a page reload fixes any of it.

**Fix.** Derive the revision from the module set actually assembled — the sorted
ids of `[...modules, ...overflow]` — rather than from `CATALOG`. The client
machinery is already correct and needs no change; it is being handed a constant
where it was promised a fingerprint.

Note that `catalog.spec.ts:380` currently asserts the revision is "built only from
things a sample cannot change". That test encodes the wrong invariant. A module
*appearing or disappearing* is not a sample changing, and it is exactly what the
revision is supposed to signal. The invariant worth keeping is narrower: no
*measurement* may move the revision. Ids may.

## 2.2 Defect B — the panel's "live" fields are not live

`Form.tsx:130` marks a control as bound when the module has a reading:

```ts
const boundAt = (path: string): TranslationKeys | undefined =>
  live == null ? undefined : BINDINGS[path]?.name;
```

A bound control is rendered read-only (`Fields.tsx:159`) and captioned with
`com_ui_system_core_live_field` — *"Driven by live data"* — plus the channel name
(`Fields.tsx:435-441`).

But the value it displays comes from `value={readPath(module, path)}`
(`Form.tsx:156` and `:186`), and `module` is the **authored** module: seeded from
the first snapshot by `catalogOf` → `draftOf` (`adapt.ts:88`), and never rewritten
afterwards by design (`useModules.ts:14-21`).

So the four bound paths — `status`, `motion.speed`, `telemetry.health`,
`telemetry.progress` — display first-poll values under a live badge, permanently.
A module can go from `running` to `fault`, recolour in the scene, recolour its
swatch in the list, and still read `running` in the form.

For contrast, the two panel elements that *are* live, and are the reason this is
easy to miss:

- the list swatch, which reads through the reading at `List.tsx:105`;
- the header chip and its "Updated Ns ago" title, at `Panel.tsx:170` and `:166`.

**Fix.** The helpers already exist and have no caller: `readoutOf`
(`resolve.ts:122`), backed by `effectiveHealth` and `effectiveProgress`
(`bind.ts:318`, `:323`). Feed the bound controls from the `Reading` when there is
one, falling back to the authored value when there is not — which is precisely
what those two functions already compute. `effectiveStatus` (`bind.ts:199`) does
the same job for the status control.

