# Part 3 — What the feed carries, and what the client keeps

## 3.1 The measurement

Taken from the captured 28-module snapshot at
`live/__tests__/__fixtures__/snapshot.json`, which was recorded from this cluster:

| | |
|---|---|
| Modules | 28 (28 catalogue, 0 discovered at capture time) |
| Channels | **91** — 88 `ok`, 3 `missing`, 0 `error` |
| Channels per module | 2 (×4), 3 (×18), 4 (×2), 5 (×3), 6 (×1) |
| By polarity | 51 `health`, 40 `activity` |
| By id | `availability` 26, `capacity` 13, `saturation` 12, `load` 11, `latency` 10, `connections` 8, `throughput` 8, `errors` 3 |
| By unit | `boolean` 26, `count` 14, `bytes` 11, `seconds` 10, `days` 10, `cores` 9, `per_second` 8, `ratio` 3 |

Each channel carries `label`, `state`, `polarity`, `value` (normalized 0..1),
`raw` (the upstream number in its unit), `unit`, and `source` (the catalogue query
id).

## 3.2 What survives the client boundary

`readingOf` (`live/adapt.ts:45`) is the whole of it:

```ts
function readingOf(m: SystemCoreModule): Reading {
  return {
    status: m.status,
    up: m.target == null ? true : m.target.up === true,
    health: finite(m.health),
    load: finite(m.load),
    rate: finite(m.rate),
    stale: m.staleness?.stale === true,
    overflow: m.origin === 'discovered',
  };
}
```

`channels[]` is not read. Nor is `staleness.scrapeAgeSeconds`, nor the rest of
`target` beyond `up`. `description`, `group`, `tags` and `dependsOn` do survive,
but through `draftOf` into the authored module — so they are captured once at
first poll, which is correct for metadata and is unrelated to Defect A.

Three numbers reach the client, and each is a lossy reduction:

- `health` = the **mean** of the health-polarity channels that resolved
  (`scale.ts:99`). Fifty-one channels collapse into one number per module, so
  which one is unwell is not recoverable. The server compensates for the worst
  case by carrying `criticalFault` separately into `deriveStatus`
  (`scale.ts:156`), which is why a root filesystem at 96% faults rather than
  averaging away — but that is a status flag, not a readable value.
- `load` = `channelValue(channels, 'load')` — the first `ok` channel with id
  `load`, normalized. Present on 11 of 28 modules. **Reaches nothing.**
- `rate` = `channelRaw(channels, 'throughput')` — the first `ok` channel with id
  `throughput`, raw. Present on 7 of 28.

**Twenty-one activity channels have no client representation at all**: all twelve
`saturation` channels (nine pod memory working sets, two "Memory used" ratios,
bytes stored), all eight `connections` channels (pods running, Mongo connections,
MySQL threads, Postgres backends, Rabbit queues, MinIO drives online, open
streams, Gitea users), and the one `activity`-polarity `errors` channel — container
restarts. The other two `errors` channels are `health` polarity, so they at least
reach `health`.

And no surface anywhere renders a raw value with its unit. The catalogue's
carefully-chosen labels — "Root filesystem free", "Certificate lifetime",
"Response time" — reach the browser on every poll and are never drawn.

## 3.3 Two channels bound to the wrong thing

Found while checking which modules have a live `rate`. Both are catalogue
mistakes, not code defects, and both are one-line changes.

**`rabbitmq.throughput` is a queue depth, not a rate.** `catalog.ts:673-680`
binds it to `kvBundle`'s `rabbitReady` = `sum(rabbitmq_queue_messages_ready)`.
That is the number of messages *waiting*, and it reaches `boundSpeed` as the
module's rate. So the RabbitMQ strip spins **faster as the queue backs up** — a
stalled consumer reads on screen as a healthy, busy service. This is the one
finding here with a genuinely misleading visual.

**`meilisearch.throughput` is a boolean.** `catalog.ts:755-762` binds it to
`meiliIndexing` = `max(meilisearch_is_indexing)`, scaled `bool`. Through the log
curve, 0 and 1 give factors of 0.85 and 0.94 — a 10% spin difference nobody will
see. The channel is honest; it is just not a throughput.

There is also a third, less serious: **`kube-state-metrics.errors` is a cumulative
counter.** `catalog.ts:498-503` binds `sum(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total)`
on a `log max 100` scale. A counter only climbs, so once the cluster has
accumulated restarts the channel saturates and never recovers. It is `activity`
polarity so it cannot cause a false fault, and it does not reach the client at
all today — but it will read wrong the moment anything displays it.
`sum(rate(...[15m]))` is the intended shape; it measures 0 on this cluster now.

