## 6.6 The strip surface: a fixed name, then a right-to-left ticker

The design: **the name stays pinned at the leading edge, `||` separates, and the
readings scroll right to left.**

```
[ REDIS || ] Commands 53.4/s || Memory used 71 MB || Availability yes ||
  ^fixed      ^a window onto a looping texture, scrolling ←
```

This dissolves the arc-budget problem. The band stops being a fixed-width label
slot and becomes a viewport onto a string of any length, so "too much data to fit"
stops being a failure mode and becomes a longer loop.

### The mechanism: scroll by texture offset, not by redrawing

Verified against `node_modules/three` at **0.184.0**:

- `Texture.offset`, `.repeat` and `.wrapS` exist (`src/textures/Texture.js:199`,
  `:210`, `:127`) and `matrixAutoUpdate` defaults to true (`:240`).
- `RepeatWrapping` is exported (`src/constants.js:555`).
- `WebGLMaterials.js:15` re-derives the uv matrix on every frame a map is used
  whenever `matrixAutoUpdate` is set — so nothing needs a `needsUpdate` flag and
  no texture data is re-uploaded.
- `CylinderGeometry` maps `u` linearly across `radialSegments`, which spans
  `thetaLength` (`src/geometries/CylinderGeometry.js:119`, `:140`) — which is why
  `Strip.tsx:135-137` can already say the UVs "run along the arc".

So `repeat.x` tiles copies of the string across the window and `offset.x` slides
them along it. **Advancing `offset.x` in `useFrame` costs one uniform write per
strip per frame and allocates nothing** — the same cost class as
`group.rotation.y += rate * delta`, which `Strip.tsx:128` already does every frame.

Version pinned deliberately: those are library internals, and a major bump could
move them.

### Five consequences that have to be designed for

**1. Two meshes, two caching rules — and the ticker must not share.**

The fixed name keeps today's shared, cache-by-string material. Its codomain is the
28 module names, so it is already bounded and sharing is a saving.

The ticker cannot share, and this is the subtle one. `LabelFactory.get` caches by
string and hands back a material carrying a **shared** `CanvasTexture`
(`labels.ts:85-146`). Two strips whose readings happened to format identically
would both write `offset.x` on the same texture every frame and scroll it at
double rate. Key the ticker cache by `id` *and* text and the hazard is impossible
by construction — and the cache is bounded at one live entry per visible module,
which is what the sweep below then collects.

**2. `LabelFactory` needs a generation sweep — and needs one today regardless.**

It is an append-only cache: one `<canvas>`, one `CanvasTexture` and one
`MeshBasicMaterial` per distinct string, freed only by `dispose()` when the scene
unmounts (`Scene.tsx:146`). `MaterialRegistry` has a generation stamp and a
`sweep()` (`materials.ts:181`, `:286`); `LabelFactory` has neither — no counter,
no eviction, no `delete`.

With a per-poll ticker string that is roughly 84 leaked textures a minute at 28
modules. Mirror `MaterialRegistry` exactly, including the two-generation grace
that lets a value vanish for one build and come back free:

```ts
export class LabelFactory {
  private readonly cache = new Map<string, THREE.MeshBasicMaterial>();
  private gen = 0;

  /** The fixed name. Shared by string — the codomain is the module names. */
  get(text: string): THREE.MeshBasicMaterial {
    return this.material('name\u0000' + text, text, false);
  }

  /**
   * The scrolling readout.
   *
   * Keyed by id as well as text so that no two strips can ever be handed the
   * same texture: `offset.x` is advanced per frame by whichever strip owns the
   * mesh, and a shared texture would be advanced once per sharer.
   */
  ticker(id: string, text: string): THREE.MeshBasicMaterial {
    return this.material('ticker\u0000' + id + '\u0000' + text, text, true);
  }

  /** Releases what the last build stopped asking for. After commit, never in
   *  render — a disposed texture still on a live mesh draws black. */
  sweep(): void {
    const floor = this.gen - 1;
    for (const [key, mat] of this.cache) {
      if ((mat.userData.gen as number) >= floor) {
        continue;
      }
      mat.map?.dispose();
      mat.dispose();
      this.cache.delete(key);
    }
    this.gen += 1;
  }
}
```

`Scene.tsx:208-210` already runs a post-commit effect for `mats.sweep()`; the
label sweep belongs on the same one.

**3. Quantise the string — for the cache, not for the pixels.**

Covered in `tickerOf` above. Every formatter branch rounds, so a module whose
numbers have not meaningfully moved yields a byte-identical key and the cache hits
without rasterising anything. Unquantised, every poll is a fresh canvas per module
even with the sweep in place — churn instead of a leak, which is better but not
free.

**4. Carry the offset across a content swap.**

When the string does change, the texture is replaced and `offset` starts at zero,
so the ticker would visibly snap back to the start every 20 seconds. Read the
outgoing offset and seed the incoming one:

```ts
const carried = tickerRef.current?.map?.offset.x ?? 0;
material.map.offset.x = carried % 1;
```

Be honest about what this does and does not buy: it removes the snap-to-start, but
it cannot preserve which word sits under the viewport, because the string itself
changed length. Continuity of motion, not continuity of content.

**5. Reduced motion must not scroll.**

`animate` is false under `prefers-reduced-motion` (`Scene.tsx:281`) and there is no
`useFrame` to advance anything, so a ticker would sit frozen mid-string and read as
broken text rather than as a still frame. **Skip the ticker mesh entirely** and let
the panel be the readout — the same call `Scene.tsx:130-141` already makes for the
audio bus, and the accessible one, since text in motion has to be pausable.

### Geometry, sizing and layout

Cost is **one additional static `cylinderGeometry` per module**. The window's arc
does not change between ticks, so nothing reconstructs; only the material swaps.

Sizing follows the same shape as the existing label math at `Strip.tsx:151-156`.
With canvas aspect `A` and world text height `lh`, one tile is `lh * A` wide in
world units, and a window spanning `winTheta` at radius `lr` has arc length
`winTheta * lr`:

```ts
const usable   = arc * (1 - 2 * LABEL_LEAD);
const nameMax  = usable * NAME_SHARE;          // name shrinks into its share
const winTheta = usable - nameTheta - sepTheta;

map.wrapS  = THREE.RepeatWrapping;
map.repeat.x = (winTheta * lr) / (lh * A);
// per frame:
map.offset.x += (SCROLL_SPEED / (lh * A)) * Math.min(delta, MAX_DELTA);
```

Two properties worth noting. The scroll rate is **independent of string length** —
a module with six channels loops for longer, it does not scroll faster, which is
what makes a stack of strips read as one instrument. And `MAX_DELTA`
(`scene/config.ts:65`) applies for the same reason it applies to rotation: returning to a
backgrounded tab must not fling the text.

Arc is still finite even if width no longer is. `LABEL_LEAD` 0.05, `LABEL_FILL`
0.95, `LABEL_H` 0.058 (`scene/config.ts:46-56`), and catalogue arcs run 50°–260°. The
name already auto-shrinks rather than squashing when it does not fit
(`Strip.tsx:151-156`), so the proposal is to split the usable arc by a fixed share,
let the name shrink into its half exactly as it does today, and **drop the ticker
on any strip whose window falls below a legibility floor**. The 50° controller ring
is the case that tests this — it carries the longest label in the catalogue
("cluster - control plane") on the narrowest arc.

There is also the trailing region the `trail` mesh occupies
(`Strip.tsx:218-224`, `arc` → `arc + min(arc * 0.8, 2.4)`) if the ticker ever wants
to run past the band's own arc rather than inside it. Not recommended for a first
pass — it would put text where there is no band behind it.

### Two calls to make looking at a real screen

Recorded as judgements, not derivations — the same standing this repo gives `GAIN`
at `catalog.ts:129-143`, which was retuned once after being looked at.

1. **Scroll rate against strip rotation.** The band is already turning: 18–67
   seconds per revolution (Part 1.2). A ticker adds a second motion to the same
   surface, and the two compound where the arc faces the camera. The rotation is
   slow enough that a readable ticker should dominate cleanly, but that is a guess
   until it is on screen. The measurement that settles it: whether a name is
   readable while its strip is at the near face.
2. **How much goes in the ticker.** At 2–6 channels a module, a full string runs
   roughly 40–110 characters. Band height is 0.05 world units — 0.02 on the
   controller — so the limit is legibility, not space. All channels is the honest
   default; a `polarity === 'health'`-only filter is the fallback if it reads as
   noise.

## 6.7 Order of work

1. **`LabelFactory` generation and sweep.** A latent leak today, independent of
   this feature, and the hard prerequisite for putting any changing string on a
   band.
2. **`Reading.channels` and the `readingOf` line.** Carries the data across the
   boundary that currently drops all 91 values.
3. **The panel readout.** Zero GPU cost, unlocks every channel at once, and makes
   step 4 designable against something real rather than imagined — the formatter
   in `ticker.ts` is shared by both, so the panel is where its output gets read
   carefully before it is 0.02 world units tall on a moving cylinder.
4. **`StripSpec.ticker`, the second mesh, and the `useFrame` offset advance.**

Steps 1–3 are independently useful and independently shippable. Only step 4
depends on all of them.

This list follows the defect fixes in Part 5.3 rather than replacing them: Defect A
in particular has to be fixed first, or a module that appears mid-session has no
strip for any of this to render on.

